// Law Academy primary-source accuracy Batch 2: holding/limits, not automatic legal sign-off.
// Append-only to existing lessons. Source notes cannot replace licensed counsel or a current citator.
(function(){'use strict';const additions=[["L3-7-9","SMITH v. ARIZONA — NARROW CONFRONTATION HOLDING (2024). When a testifying expert recounts an absent forensic analyst's factual statements to support an opinion, and those statements support the opinion only if true, the statements are introduced for their truth; calling them merely the basis of the expert opinion does not avoid the Sixth Amendment inquiry. The Court vacated and remanded without deciding whether the particular out-of-court statements were testimonial; that factual and doctrinal question must be analyzed independently. Apply the rule to the recorded forensic conclusion offered through a non-testing colleague. For prosecution, identify any independent testing or genuinely nontruth purpose; for defense, identify each substituted analyst statement and its necessary truth use. Change the record so the testifying analyst performs a fresh independent analysis without relaying the absent analyst's factual assertions. Submit a statement-level chart and two proposed admissibility rulings, expressly distinguishing truth use, testimonial character, unavailability and prior cross-examination. Primary opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-899. No later-treatment certification."],["L3-1-5","FORENSIC HEARSAY AND CONFRONTATION CROSSCHECK — In a prosecution, the hearsay exceptions and expert basis rule do not themselves answer a Sixth Amendment objection. Smith v. Arizona (2024) explains that an expert's retelling of an absent analyst's statements is for their truth if the opinion depends on the statements being true; the Court did not decide on the record whether the statements were testimonial. Identify every out-of-court assertion, its declarant, its forensic purpose, whether it is offered for truth, and whether the accused had an opportunity to confront the original maker if testimonial. Distinguish physical evidence authentication, layered hearsay, and independent expert analysis. Make separate objections and responses for an absent analyst's notes, the substitute expert's new measurements and a machine-generated timestamp without a human assertion. Primary opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-899. Further local authority review PENDING."],["L4-2-4","TRIAL IDENTIFICATION AND SUBSTITUTE-EXPERT LIMIT — A confrontation objection must focus on the statement and the witness against the accused, not merely the fact that a forensic expert has professional credentials. Under Smith v. Arizona (2024), an absent analyst's factual assertions recounted to support another expert's opinion are offered for truth when their truth is necessary to the opinion. Whether those assertions are testimonial remains a distinct contested issue, which Smith did not resolve on its particular record. Test that doctrine separately from due-process challenges to suggestive identification and from ordinary evidentiary foundation. For both parties prepare an exhibit-by-exhibit table: actual declarant, asserted fact, truth purpose, primary purpose, available confrontation and alternative witnesses. Change the record to an expert who re-tests retained material and reaches an independent conclusion without reciting the absent analyst's statements. Primary source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-899. Source date and later court treatment must be rechecked before grading."],["L5-4-2","SMITH v. ARIZONA FORENSIC-REPORT LIMIT — The Supreme Court ruled in 2024 that the expert-basis label cannot shield an absent analyst's statements when a substitute expert's opinion depends on their truth; the Court reserved whether the particular statements were testimonial on remand. A criminal trial memo must therefore test: (1) the precise factual assertions supplied by a non-testifying analyst; (2) whether the prosecution offers them for their truth; (3) the testimonial primary-purpose inquiry; (4) availability and prior cross-examination, if the statements are testimonial; and (5) a potentially lawful independent expert or nontruth alternative. Defendant argues that a surrogate cannot repeat the necessary lab assertions without confrontation. Prosecutor may argue a genuinely independent conclusion or that the original statements were not testimonial; neither claim is automatically dispositive. Draft a motion and response, examination script, and a statement-by-statement proposed ruling. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-899. No subsequent-treatment sign-off."],["L5-4-7","FORENSIC SUBSTITUTE EXPERT — NARROW SOURCE NOTE. Smith v. Arizona (2024) rejects admission of an absent analyst's truth-dependent assertions merely as another expert's basis evidence. It does not make every machine output a testimonial human statement, decide all primary-purpose questions, or prohibit every genuinely independent expert conclusion. Determine separately whether the surrogate reports another analyst's observations, merely relies on a permissible independently admissible source, or performs new measurements and explains independent results. The decisive question about testimonial character was left unresolved on Smith's record. Compare the outcome if the first analyst prepared litigation-directed notes versus routine machine calibration data. Produce a forensic-statement chain, opposing constitutional motions and a conditional evidentiary order. Primary source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-899; current controlling application requires separate review."],["L3-3-4","SEC v. JARKESY — JURY TRIAL BOUNDARY. The 2024 Supreme Court decision held that when the SEC seeks civil penalties for securities fraud, the Seventh Amendment entitles the defendant to a jury trial; the Court rejected applying the public-rights exception to that particular action. It did not categorically invalidate all agency adjudications, every administrative civil penalty, or factual hearings under APA §§ 554–557. In a mock administrative case, isolate the statutory cause of action, common-law analogue, requested legal or equitable remedy, and whether a historically recognized public-rights category applies. Government argues a distinct regulatory scheme involves a public right; respondent compares punitive fraud penalties with common-law adjudication. Change the facts from securities-fraud civil penalties to a licensing determination without the same cause or remedy. Deliver a Seventh Amendment classification memo and competing forum proposals. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-859. Later applications PENDING."],["L3-3-5","AGENCY ENFORCEMENT FORUM CHECK — SEC v. Jarkesy (2024) addressed the SEC's effort to impose civil penalties for securities fraud in an in-house adjudication and found a Seventh Amendment jury right. The public-rights inquiry turns on the actual claim and remedy rather than a blanket rule that every administrative enforcement proceeding is unconstitutional. Compare a requested punitive penalty for a fraud-like cause with a prospective licensing or technical compliance order. Separately analyze the underlying enabling statute, any required exhaustion, final agency action, statutory hearing rights, and available judicial review; do not merge those procedural questions into the Seventh Amendment issue. Prepare a claim/remedy/forum matrix, opposing legal analyses, and a proposed alternative enforcement path with primary citation: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-859. Current subsequent treatment remains subject to independent checking."],["L4-5-3","REGULATORY PENALTY AND HEARING — JARKESY LIMITATION. When evaluating whether an agency may settle or adjudicate an enforcement penalty, classify the underlying claim and remedy. SEC v. Jarkesy (2024) holds that defendants facing SEC civil penalties for securities fraud have a Seventh Amendment right to a jury; the Court's analysis did not abolish administrative settlements or every nonjury agency hearing. Explain whether the regulator is seeking money resembling legal punitive damages, an administrative license consequence, a consent order, or equitable corrective relief, and the source of each proposed enforcement power. Propose lawful settlement options without representing every compromise as a jury-right waiver. Draft a rights-notice chart, contested-hearing alternatives and a record-based recommendation. Opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-859. Verify actual agency statute and later rulings."],["L5-1-3","TRUMP v. CASA — RELIEF SCOPE AND MERITS SEPARATION. On June 27, 2025, in emergency stay applications, the Supreme Court concluded the universal preliminary injunctions at issue likely exceeded federal courts' equitable authority under the Judiciary Act, insofar as they protected nonparties beyond what was needed to give complete relief to the plaintiffs. It did not decide the legality of the challenged birthright-citizenship executive order. In the assigned injunction hypothetical, determine the named plaintiffs, their injuries and the relief needed to make them whole; then separately examine potential class-action procedures and any statutory remedies, without assuming that every geographically broad impact is itself forbidden. Government argues nonparty protection exceeds traditional equity; plaintiffs argue a particular breadth is necessary to furnish full plaintiff relief. Produce alternative tailored orders and a judicial opinion that explicitly leaves underlying merits for proper adjudication. Primary majority opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24A884. Further 2026 case treatment requires separate checks."],["L4-4-9","EMERGENCY RELIEF — CASA PROCEDURAL DISTINCTION. Trump v. CASA (2025) addressed applications for partial stays of injunctions extending beyond litigants; the Supreme Court found that the orders' universal reach likely exceeded federal equitable authority under the Judiciary Act. The Court did not adjudicate the underlying citizenship merits. A stay motion still requires the applicable stay standard, an account of the named party's injuries, a tailored remedy, and attention to the actual record and jurisdiction. Appellant may propose narrowing the order to complete party relief; appellee should explain, if supportable, why narrower relief would not fully remedy the established plaintiff harm. Change facts from two individual plaintiffs to a properly certified class and analyze the separate certification basis before writing a proposed alternative order. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24A884. No universal proposition about all statutory vacatur follows automatically."],["L4-4-10","CONSTITUTIONAL APPELLATE FINAL — CASA HOLDING LIMIT. To test emergency remedy selection, separate reviewability and standing, preliminary merits, likelihood of irreparable injury, and actual power to grant relief extending to nonparties. Trump v. CASA (2025) arose on applications for partial stays; the Supreme Court held the contested universal injunctions likely exceeded equitable authority and did not decide the underlying citizenship merits. The final response must not announce a merits victory as though CASA resolved it. Write a tailored order for the named plaintiffs, a second proposal analyzing permissible class-related relief where procedure supports it, and a counterargument addressing administrability and complete relief. Identify exactly which part of the requested relief would bind officials and why. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24A884. Additional forum-specific precedent and subsequent history must be checked."]];
const all=[...(window.LAW_LEVEL3||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[]),...(window.LAW_LEVEL4||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[]),...(window.LAW_LEVEL5||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[])];
const byId=new Map(all.map(l=>[l.id,l]));if(byId.size!==310)throw Error('Advanced law levels not fully loaded');
for(const [id,note] of additions){const l=byId.get(id);if(!l||typeof l.reading!=='string')throw Error('Missing legal-accuracy lesson '+id);if(!l.reading.includes(note))l.reading+='\n\nPRIMARY-AUTHORITY HOLDING/LIMIT CROSSCHECK — '+note;}
window.LAW_CROSSLEVEL_PRIMARY_SOURCE_BATCH2_IDS=additions.map(([id])=>id);
})();